  <static seed="2008" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/Percent/MTH20PercentApplicationType2_120.pg">
    <statement><p>A community college conducted a survey about the number of students riding each bus line available. The following bar graph is the result of the survey.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/section-percentages-5-5-27-image-1.png" width="66%"/>
<p>What percent of students ride Bus #1?</p>
<p>Approximately <fillin characters="9" name="AnSwEr0001"/> of students ride Bus #1.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>15\%</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>First, we find the total number of students who participated in this survey:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 93+51+167+150+144=605 }</m></p>
<p>Now, this problem can be boiled down to this question: <m>93</m> is what percent of <m>605</m>? We will show multiple methods to solve this problem.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 1</p>
<p>We will use proportion to solve this problem.</p>
<p>Assume <m>93</m> is <m>x\%</m> of <m>605</m>, so <lq/><m>x</m> out of <m>100</m><rq/> corresponds to <lq/><m>93</m> out of <m>605</m><rq/>.</p>
<p>We will write and solve the proportion:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}[t]
   \frac{x}{100} \amp = \frac{93}{605} \\
   605x \amp = 93 \cdot 100 \\
   605x \amp = 9300 \\
   \frac{605x}{605} \amp = \frac{9300}{605} \\
   x \amp \approx 15.3719008264463 \\
   x \amp \approx 15\%
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>Approximately <m>15</m> of students ride Bus #1.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 2</p>
<p>We will use the percentage formula to solve this problem.</p>
<p>This translation from English to may  should help you remember the percentage formula.</p>
<p><m>2 \text{ is } 50\% \text{ of } 4 \Longleftrightarrow 2 = 0.5 \cdot 4</m></p>
<p>Assume <m>93</m> is <m>x</m> (as a percent) of <m>605</m>. We have:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}
  93 \amp = x \cdot 605 \\
  \frac{93}{605} \amp = \frac{x \cdot 605}{605} \\  
  0.153719008264463 \amp \approx x \\
  x \amp \approx 15\%
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>Approximately <m>15</m> of students ride Bus #1.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 3</p>
<p>In the sentence <lq/><m>93</m> is what percent of <m>605</m>,<rq/></p>
<p><ul label="disc">
<li>
<p><m>93</m> is the <alert>percentage</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><lq/>what percent<rq/> is the <alert>rate</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>605</m> is the <alert>base</alert> (following the word <lq/>of<rq/>).</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p>By the formula <m>\text{rate} = \frac{\text{percentage}}{\text{base}}</m>, we do a division to solve the problem:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \text{rate} = \frac{\text{percentage}}{\text{base}} = \frac{93}{605} \approx 0.153719008264463 \approx 15\% }</m></p>
<p>Approximately <m>15</m> of students ride Bus #1.</p>
</solution>
